The design and implementation of programming languages is one of the oldest and most enduring intellectual pursuits in computer science. From the earliest days of electronic computation---when programmers wrote raw machine code in binary---to the present era of multi-stage compilers, dependent type systems, and self-optimizing runtimes, the central challenge remains the same: to provide precise, efficient, and expressive means for humans to communicate computational ideas to machines.

This book is a comprehensive treatise on that challenge. It covers the full spectrum of programming language development, from the mathematical foundations that underpin all language theory, through the design of syntax and semantics, the construction of compilers and interpreters, to the cutting edge of modern compiler architectures. Our approach is deliberately theoretical: we believe that a deep understanding of the mathematical structures underlying programming languages is not a luxury but a necessity.

