\section{The Landscape of Programming Language Development}\label{sec:intro-landscape}

The landscape of programming language development is vast. At the time of this writing, there are thousands of programming languages in active use, ranging from low-level systems languages like C~\cite{aho2007compilers} and Rust, through general-purpose languages like Java, Python, and Go, to domain-specific languages embedded in scientific computing, web development, and hardware description.

Despite this diversity, all programming languages share a common structure. Every language must address three fundamental concerns:

\begin{definition}[Syntax]\label{def:syntax}
The \emph{syntax} of a programming language is the set of rules governing the form of valid programs. It defines what sequences of symbols constitute well-formed expressions, statements, and declarations. Syntax is typically divided into \emph{lexical structure} (the formation of tokens from characters) and \emph{phrase structure} (the formation of phrases from tokens).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Semantics]\label{def:semantics}
The \emph{semantics} of a programming language is the meaning assigned to syntactically valid programs. It defines what computation a program performs when executed. Formal semantics provides mathematically precise definitions of meaning, free from the ambiguities of natural language description.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pragmatics]\label{def:pragmatics}
The \emph{pragmatics} of a programming language encompasses all aspects of language use that are not captured by syntax or semantics alone: efficiency of implementation, error reporting, tooling, ecosystem, community conventions, and the overall programmer experience.
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
The trichotomy of syntax, semantics, and pragmatics is not merely a pedagogical convenience. It reflects a deep structural fact about language design: syntax is the \emph{form} of expression, semantics is the \emph{meaning} of expression, and pragmatics is the \emph{use} of expression. Each aspect can be studied independently, but a successful language requires all three to be well-designed and mutually coherent.
\end{insight}

