Modern compilers often deviate from this linear pipeline in important ways. Just-in-time (JIT) compilers interleave compilation and execution~\cite{wurthinger2017truffle}. Multi-stage compilers apply transformations at multiple levels of abstraction~\cite{lattner2021mlir}. Incremental compilers recompile only the parts of a program that have changed~\cite{}. The classical pipeline remains a useful conceptual framework, but the reality is more nuanced.

\section{Key Concepts: Syntax, Semantics, and Pragmatics}\label{sec:intro-key-concepts}

\subsection{Syntax and Grammars}\label{sec:intro-syntax}

The syntax of a programming language is typically specified using a formal grammar. The most common approach is to use a context-free grammar (CFG), which defines the phrase structure of the language. A CFG $G = (N, \Sigma, P, S)$ consists of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A set $N$ of nonterminal symbols (syntactic categories)
  \item A set $\Sigma$ of terminal symbols (tokens)
  \item A set $P$ of production rules of the form $A \to \alpha$, where $A \in N$ and $\alpha \in (N \cup \Sigma)^*$
  \item A start symbol $S \in N$
\end{itemize}

\begin{grammar}{Simple Expressions}\label{gram:intro-expr}
E & \longrightarrow E \mathbin{+} E \mid E \mathbin{*} E \mid \textbf{(} \; E \; \textbf{)} \mid n
\end{grammar}

The grammar above defines a simple language of arithmetic expressions. The nonterminals are $\{E\}$, the terminals include $\{+, *, (, ), n\}$ (where $n$ represents any integer literal), and the productions define how expressions are formed.

\subsection{Formal Semantics}\label{sec:intro-semantics}

There are three main approaches to formal semantics, each providing a different perspective on the meaning of programs:

\begin{definition}[Operational Semantics]\label{def:op-sem}
\emph{Operational semantics} defines the meaning of a program by specifying how it executes on an abstract machine. It comes in two flavors:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Big-step semantics} (also called \emph{natural semantics}) defines the final result of evaluating a program, written $e \evalto v$ (``expression $e$ evaluates to value $v$'').
  \item \textbf{Small-step semantics} (also called \emph{structural operational semantics}) defines individual computation steps, written $e \stepsto e'$ (``expression $e$ reduces in one step to $e'$'').
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Denotational Semantics]\label{def:denot-sem}
\emph{Denotational semantics} defines the meaning of a program by mapping it to a mathematical object---typically a function in a domain of continuous functions. The meaning of a program is its \emph{denotation}: a mathematical value that captures its computational content~\cite{winskel1993semantics}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Axiomatic Semantics]\label{def:axiom-sem}
\emph{Axiomatic semantics} defines the meaning of a program indirectly, by specifying logical assertions about its execution. The central construct is the \emph{Hoare triple} $\{P\}\;c\;\{Q\}$, which asserts that if precondition $P$ holds before command $c$ executes, then postcondition $Q$ holds afterward.
\end{definition}

Each approach has its strengths. Operational semantics is intuitive and directly executable. Denotational semantics is compositional and mathematically elegant. Axiomatic semantics is oriented toward verification. In practice, all three are used, often in combination.

\subsection{Type Systems}\label{sec:intro-types}

A type system is a syntactic method for proving the absence of certain program behaviors, by classifying phrases according to the kinds of values they compute~\cite{pierce2002types}. Type systems are among the most important practical innovations in programming language design.

\begin{insight}
The fundamental purpose of a type system is not to ensure that programs are ``correct''---that is a far too ambitious goal. Rather, a type system establishes a \emph{safety property}: a guarantee that certain kinds of errors (type errors) cannot occur during execution. This is a precise, mathematically provable guarantee, and it is what makes type systems so valuable.
\end{insight}

Type systems range from simple (the untyped lambda calculus has no types at all; the simply-typed lambda calculus has base types and function types) to extraordinarily sophisticated (dependent type systems where types can depend on values, linear type systems that track resource usage, session types that verify communication protocols).

\section{The Role of Theory in Practice}\label{sec:intro-theory-practice}

A common misconception is that theoretical computer science is divorced from practical programming. Nothing could be further from the truth. Every major advance in compiler technology has been driven by theoretical insights:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Formal grammars and parsing} (Chomsky, Backus, Knuth) led to parser generators like Yacc and ANTLR, which are used in virtually every compiler.
  \item \textbf{Abstract interpretation} (Cousot and Cousot, 1977~\cite{cousot1977abstract}) provides the theoretical foundation for all static program analysis, from constant propagation to pointer analysis.
  \item \textbf{Type theory} (Curry, Howard, Martin-L\"of, Girard) underlies the type systems of ML, Haskell, Rust, and many other modern languages.
  \item \textbf{Category theory} provides the mathematical framework for understanding monads, functors, and algebraic effects---concepts that have entered mainstream programming through Haskell, Scala, and Swift.
  \item \textbf{Proof theory and the Curry--Howard correspondence} enable verified compilation (CompCert~\cite{leroy2009compcert}) and proof assistants (Coq, Agda, Lean).
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The relationship between theory and practice in programming language development has evolved through several phases. In the 1950s and 1960s, language design was largely ad hoc, driven by hardware constraints and the needs of scientific computing. The 1970s saw the emergence of formal methods: structured programming, denotational semantics, and the first type systems. The 1980s and 1990s brought a maturation of theory: abstract interpretation, type inference, and module systems. The 2000s and 2010s witnessed the application of theory at scale: verified compilers, dependently typed languages, and multi-core compilation. Today, theory and practice are more intertwined than ever.
\end{historical}

\section{A Brief History of Programming Languages}\label{sec:intro-history}

The history of programming languages is a rich tapestry of ideas, innovations, and intellectual cross-pollination. We provide only the briefest sketch here; the full history is told in \chref{history-langs}.

\subsection{The First Generation: Machine Code and Assembly (1940s--1950s)}

The earliest programs were written in raw machine code---sequences of binary digits representing hardware instructions. Assembly languages, which use mnemonic names for instructions, emerged in the early 1950s. These were not ``programming languages'' in the modern sense, but they established the fundamental concept of symbolic representation of computation.

\subsection{Fortran and the Birth of Compilation (1957)}

Fortran (Formula Translation), developed by John Backus and his team at IBM in 1957, was the first widely used high-level programming language. It introduced the concept of compilation: translating a high-level language into machine code. The Fortran compiler was a landmark achievement, demonstrating that high-level languages could produce efficient code.

\subsection{Lisp and the Lambda Calculus (1958)}

John McCarthy's Lisp (List Processing), introduced in 1958, was based on the lambda calculus---a formal system invented by Alonzo Church in the 1930s. Lisp introduced garbage collection, recursive functions, and the concept of a program as a data structure (homoiconicity). It remains in active use today through dialects like Scheme and Common Lisp.

\subsection{The Structured Programming Era (1960s--1970s)}

The 1960s saw the development of several influential languages:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{ALGOL 60} introduced block structure, lexical scoping, and Backus--Naur Form (BNF) for syntax description.
  \item \textbf{C} (1972) combined low-level access with high-level control structures, becoming the dominant systems programming language.
  \item \textbf{Prolog} (1972) introduced logic programming, based on first-order logic.
  \item \textbf{ML} (1973) introduced polymorphic type inference and algebraic data types.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Object-Oriented Revolution (1980s--1990s)}

The 1980s and 1990s saw the rise of object-oriented programming:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{C++} (1985) added object-oriented features to C.
  \item \textbf{Java} (1995) popularized garbage collection, virtual machines, and platform independence.
  \item \textbf{Python} (1991) emphasized readability and dynamic typing.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Modern Era (2000s--Present)}

The 21st century has seen an explosion of innovation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Rust} (2010) introduced ownership and borrowing for memory safety without garbage collection.
  \item \textbf{Go} (2009) emphasized simplicity and concurrency.
  \item \textbf{Swift} (2014) combined safety, performance, and modern language design.
  \item \textbf{Lean} (2013) pushed dependent types toward practical theorem proving.
\end{itemize}

\section{Reading Guide and Prerequisites}\label{sec:intro-prereqs}

\subsection{Prerequisites}

This book assumes the following background:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Programming experience}: You should be comfortable writing programs in at least one language. Familiarity with functional programming (Haskell, OCaml, or Scheme) is helpful but not required.
  \item \textbf{Discrete mathematics}: Basic familiarity with sets, functions, relations, and proof techniques (induction, contradiction) is essential.
  \item \item \textbf{Automata theory}: Familiarity with finite automata and regular expressions is helpful for Parts~IV and~VII.
  \item \textbf{Mathematical maturity}: The ability to read and write formal definitions, theorems, and proofs is essential. This book is not a popular exposition; it is a technical treatise.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{How to Read This Book}

The book is designed to be read in multiple ways:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Cover to cover}: The reader who proceeds from Part~I through Part~XIV will acquire a thorough understanding of the field. This is the recommended approach for graduate students and researchers.
  
  \item \textbf{By part}: Each part is relatively self-contained. A practitioner interested in compiler optimization can read Parts~VI--VIII without reading all of Part~I. However, the mathematical foundations in Part~I are referenced throughout.
  
  \item \textbf{By topic}: Individual chapters can be read independently. The cross-references and index will guide you to prerequisite material as needed.
\end{enumerate}

