\section{The Compiler Pipeline in Detail}\label{sec:intro-pipeline-detail}

A modern compiler is a complex piece of software, typically consisting of hundreds of thousands of lines of code. Despite this complexity, the compilation process can be decomposed into a sequence of well-defined phases, each with a clear responsibility.

\subsection{Front End: Analysis}\label{sec:intro-frontend}

The \emph{front end} of a compiler is responsible for analyzing the source program and checking that it is well-formed. It typically consists of:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Lexical analysis} (scanning): The source text is decomposed into a sequence of \emph{tokens}---the basic syntactic units of the language (identifiers, keywords, operators, literals, etc.).
  
  \item \textbf{Syntactic analysis} (parsing): The sequence of tokens is organized into an \emph{abstract syntax tree} (AST) according to the grammar of the language.
  
  \item \textbf{Semantic analysis}: The AST is checked for semantic consistency: types are checked, names are resolved, scoping rules are enforced, and other language-specific constraints are verified.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Middle End: Optimization}\label{sec:intro-middle-end}

The \emph{middle end} transforms the program to improve its performance. It operates on an \emph{intermediate representation} (IR) that is independent of both the source language and the target machine. Key activities include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Dataflow analysis}: Computing properties of the program by analyzing the flow of data through the control-flow graph.
  \item \textbf{Optimization passes}: Transforming the IR to eliminate redundant computations, hoist invariants out of loops, inline function calls, and so on.
  \item \textbf{Abstract interpretation}: Systematically approximating program properties to enable more aggressive optimization.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Back End: Synthesis}\label{sec:intro-backend}

The \emph{back end} generates code for the target machine. It typically consists of:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Instruction selection}: Choosing target machine instructions to implement each IR operation.
  \item \textbf{Register allocation}: Assigning program variables to a finite set of hardware registers.
  \item \textbf{Instruction scheduling}: Ordering instructions to maximize pipeline utilization and minimize stalls.
\end{enumerate}

